findings.body:
`ReactScan` renders `null`; it only starts a side effect. Even so, the loader shows a real `<div>` with the text "Loading devtools..." while the chunk loads:

::diff{file="web/app/components/devtools/react-scan/loader.tsx" lines="19-23"}

This is rendered as the first child of `<body>` in `layout.tsx`, before `ThemeProvider`. In development, every full page load briefly shows this stray text, and the layout shifts when it disappears. It also uses `text-gray-500` instead of the project's semantic text tokens. The TanStack loader already uses `fallback={null}`, and the same choice works here:

::suggestion{file="web/app/components/devtools/react-scan/loader.tsx" lines="20-20"}
    <Suspense fallback={null}>

The React Scan loader logs when its dynamic import fails, but the TanStack loader drops the error without a trace:

::diff{file="web/app/components/devtools/tanstack/loader.tsx" lines="6-12"}

Before this PR, a failed import here would surface as an error. Now, if a `@tanstack/*-devtools` package is missing or broken, the devtools panel just never appears and there's nothing in the console to explain why. That's a frustrating dev-only failure to debug. Both loaders should behave the same way:

::suggestion{file="web/app/components/devtools/tanstack/loader.tsx" lines="9-11"}
  })).catch((error) => {
    console.error('Failed to load TanStack devtools:', error)
    return { default: () => null }

The description says the PR "Simplified `web/app/components/devtools/index.ts` using clean exports". No such file exists. `devtools/` contains only the `react-scan/` and `tanstack/` folders, and both consumers deep-import the loaders:

::diff{file="web/context/query-client.tsx" lines="5-5"}

Nothing breaks today, since nothing imports `@/app/components/devtools` any more. Still, either add the barrel (exporting only the loaders, so the heavy implementation files stay out of the static graph) or correct the description.

Before this PR, `layout.tsx` imported `ReactScan` statically, so `react-scan` shipped in every production client bundle even though `scan()` only ran under `IS_DEV`. Now the import is dynamic, behind `if (!IS_DEV) return null`, and `IS_DEV` is a compile-time `process.env.NODE_ENV` comparison. Production builds can therefore tree-shake that dead branch out, so react-scan is no longer in the production bundle.

::code{file="web/app/components/devtools/react-scan/loader.tsx" lines="6-18"}

The files were moved as pure renames (100% similarity), which keeps the diff easy to review. The inner `IS_DEV` check in `scan.tsx` is now redundant but harmless.
